---
/*
 * dynamics-lab.astro — an instrument for looking at integrators, not a published page.
 *
 * noindex and unlinked, in the mould of /star-render-lab. What it is FOR is the one question the
 * three schemes in `core/dynamics` disagree about: how the timestep is chosen, and what that
 * costs. Everything on the page is either a control over that or a readout of its consequence.
 *
 * The energy readout is the point. A cluster that looks plausible while its energy drifts by 90%
 * is the failure mode this whole module is built against, and it is invisible in the picture —
 * the stars still move, the cluster still looks like a cluster. So the number is on screen at all
 * times, next to the thing it judges, and it latches: a run that misbehaves and recovers stays
 * marked, because the recovery does not undo what happened.
 */
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import PageHeader from "../components/PageHeader.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Dynamics lab — Anna Rosen"
  description="Development surface: N-body integrators, softening, and what conservation costs."
  path="/dynamics-lab"
  noindex
>
  <PageHeader
    eyebrow="Development surface · not linked"
    title="How you choose the timestep is the physics"
    lede="Three integrators on identical initial conditions. Same stars, same masses, same softening — the only difference is how each decides how far to step. Watch the energy, not the picture."
  />

  <div class="wrap-wide lab">
    <div class="lab__stage">
      <canvas
        class="lab__canvas"
        data-canvas
        aria-label="A star cluster of a few hundred stars, integrated in real time."></canvas>
      <p class="lab__note" data-status aria-live="polite">Starting…</p>
    </div>

    <div class="lab__side flow">
      <fieldset class="ctl">
        <legend>Integrator</legend>
        <label class="ctl__row">
          <input type="radio" name="scheme" value="fsi4" checked />
          <span><strong>FSI4</strong> — 4th-order forward symplectic, fixed step</span>
        </label>
        <label class="ctl__row">
          <input type="radio" name="scheme" value="hermite" />
          <span><strong>Hermite</strong> — 4th-order predictor-corrector, <em>adaptive</em></span>
        </label>
        <label class="ctl__row">
          <input type="radio" name="scheme" value="leapfrog" />
          <span><strong>Leapfrog</strong> — 2nd-order symplectic, fixed step</span>
        </label>
      </fieldset>

      <fieldset class="ctl">
        <legend>Softening <span class="ctl__val" data-eps-label>0.5 d</span></legend>
        <input type="range" data-eps min="0" max="5" step="1" value="0" />
        <p class="ctl__hint">
          ε = fraction · r<sub>h</sub> · N<sup>−1/3</sup>. Smaller ε means more of the Coulomb
          logarithm, so more real two-body relaxation — and a shorter step to resolve it.
        </p>
      </fieldset>

      <fieldset class="ctl">
        <legend>Stars <span class="ctl__val" data-n-label>200</span></legend>
        <input type="range" data-n min="100" max="600" step="50" value="200" />
        <p class="ctl__hint">O(N²). Measured interactive ceiling is around 512.</p>
      </fieldset>

      <div class="ctl ctl--buttons">
        <button type="button" data-play aria-pressed="true">Pause</button>
        <button type="button" data-reset>Reset</button>
      </div>

      <dl class="meter" data-meter>
        <div class="meter__row"><dt>|ΔE/E|</dt><dd data-de>—</dd></div>
        <div class="meter__row"><dt>worst |ΔE/E|</dt><dd data-dew>—</dd></div>
        <div class="meter__row"><dt>Q = T/|U|</dt><dd data-q>—</dd></div>
        <div class="meter__row"><dt>t / t<sub>cross</sub></dt><dd data-tc>—</dd></div>
        <div class="meter__row"><dt>bound</dt><dd data-bound>—</dd></div>
        <div class="meter__row"><dt>force evals / frame</dt><dd data-cost>—</dd></div>
      </dl>
      <p class="meter__verdict" data-health>—</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="prose flow">
      <h2>What to look for</h2>
      <p>
        Drag the softening down. Measured over ten crossing times at N = 200, seed 2026 — worst
        |ΔE/E| at each rung:
      </p>
      <table class="tbl">
        <caption>Worst energy drift over 10 t<sub>cross</sub>, identical initial conditions.</caption>
        <thead>
          <tr
            ><th scope="col">ε / r<sub>h</sub></th><th scope="col">FSI4</th><th scope="col"
              >Hermite</th
            ><th scope="col">Leapfrog</th><th scope="col">Hermite cost</th></tr
          >
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><th scope="row">0.086</th><td>1.3e−5</td><td>8.3e−7</td><td>7.5e−3</td><td>765</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">0.043</th><td>7.3e−4</td><td>4.6e−6</td><td>1.4e−1</td><td>1333</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">0.021</th><td>1.3e−2</td><td>1.3e−5</td><td>7.8e−2</td><td>1928</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">0.011</th><td>6.2e+0</td><td>2.3e−5</td><td>1.6e+0</td><td>2360</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">0.005</th><td>1.1e+0</td><td>5.1e−5</td><td>2.4e+0</td><td>2532</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">0.003</th><td>6.6e+1</td><td>5.7e−4</td><td>2.4e+0</td><td>9809</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p>
        The two fixed-step schemes fail, but not at the same place and not tidily. The leapfrog is
        already at 14% by ε = 0.043 r<sub>h</sub>; FSI4 is still at 7.3 × 10<sup>−4</sup> there and
        does not cross 10% until nearer 0.02. <strong
          >Neither column is monotonic</strong
        > — FSI4 runs 6.2, then 1.1, then 66. That is not noise in the measurement, it is the
        answer: past roughly 128 steps per crossing time the residual is no longer the timestep
        but individual close encounters, which are chaotic and do not average down. A run that
        happens to avoid a hard pass looks better than a finer one that does not.
      </p>
      <p>
        Hermite holds 10<sup>−5</sup> or better across almost the whole range, reaching 5.7 ×
        10<sup>−4</sup> only at the finest rung, because it shortens its own step when a pair
        closes. It buys that with work — the last column is force evaluations per crossing time,
        and it rises 13× across the scan. Nothing here is free, and the trade is the subject.
      </p>
      <p>
        The deeper point, which the slider can only half show: <strong
          >Hermite does not actually need the softening at all</strong
        >. Run at ε = 0 exactly it conserves to 3.4 × 10<sup>−5</sup> — better than any softened
        rung above — because an adaptive step is the mechanism that handles a close pair, and
        softening is the substitute a fixed step is forced to use instead. FSI4 at ε = 0 does not
        converge at any step this page can afford: 1.5 × 10<sup>3</sup> at 128 steps per crossing
        time, still 0.37 at 8192. The softening on this page is scaffolding for the fixed-step
        schemes, not physics, and the ladder's lowest rungs describe those schemes failing rather
        than anything about stellar dynamics.
      </p>
      <p>
        Softening is not a nuisance parameter to be minimised. It sets which encounters the model
        represents at all — ε cuts the Coulomb logarithm off at ln(r<sub>h</sub>/ε), so a large ε
        suppresses the two-body relaxation this force model exists to produce, and a small one
        demands a step the integrator has to be able to take.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

<script>
  import {
    availableSchemes,
    chooseIntegrator,
    createConservationMonitor,
    createDirectForce,
    crossingTime,
    measure,
    softeningForCluster,
    stepsForSoftening,
    clusterState,
    type ConservationMonitor,
    type ForceModel,
    type Integrator,
    type Scheme,
    type State,
    type Vec3Array,
  } from "@novascope/core/dynamics";
  import { defaultIdentity } from "@novascope/core/cluster/params";

  /* Every lookup goes through one throwing helper, so the rest of the file works with non-null
     types. Returning null and checking at each use would let a missing element degrade into a
     silently half-working page, which on an instrument is worse than not loading. */
  const el = <T extends Element>(sel: string): T => {
    const found = document.querySelector<T>(sel);
    if (!found) throw new Error(`dynamics-lab: ${sel} missing`);
    return found;
  };

  const canvas = el<HTMLCanvasElement>("[data-canvas]");
  const status = el<HTMLElement>("[data-status]");
  const maybeCtx = canvas.getContext("2d");
  if (!maybeCtx) throw new Error("dynamics-lab: no 2d context");
  /* Bound to an explicitly typed const so the narrowing survives into the draw closures — the
     same reason `core/dynamics/fsi4.ts` binds its narrowed force model rather than asserting. */
  const ctx: CanvasRenderingContext2D = maybeCtx;
  const playBtn = el<HTMLButtonElement>("[data-play]");
  const resetBtn = el<HTMLButtonElement>("[data-reset]");
  const epsInput = el<HTMLInputElement>("[data-eps]");
  const nInput = el<HTMLInputElement>("[data-n]");

  /* The softening ladder is discrete and labelled in units of d = r_h N^(-1/3), because that is
     the quantity the physics is stated in. A free slider in parsecs would let a reader set a value
     with no relationship to the cluster it is softening. */
  const FRACTIONS = [0.5, 0.25, 0.125, 0.0625, 0.03125, 0.015625];

  const SCALE_PC = 0.5;
  const R_HALF = SCALE_PC * 1.305; // Plummer: r_h = 1.305 a
  /* The drawn half-width is DERIVED from the cluster, not chosen: 4 half-mass radii, which holds
     the bound cluster and a margin of escapers. A hardcoded value drew a 0.65 pc cluster inside a
     6 pc field, i.e. filling 11% of the frame, and the fix for that is the right number rather
     than a zoom that would drift out of step with the profile. */
  const VIEW_PC = 4 * R_HALF;

  /** How far one animation frame advances the clock. Also the unit the delivered rate is in. */
  const FRAMES_PER_TCROSS = 64;

  interface Run {
    state: State;
    force: ForceModel;
    integrator: Integrator;
    monitor: ConservationMonitor;
    tCross: number;
    scheme: Scheme;
    evals: () => number;
    /**
     * Sub-steps per crossing time this run will ACTUALLY take, or null when the scheme sizes
     * its own and the answer is not knowable in advance.
     *
     * Derived rather than echoed. `stepsForSoftening` returns what the softening ASKS for, and
     * the fixed-step schemes subdivide each frame by `ceil(dt / maxStep)` — an integer — so the
     * delivered rate is the request rounded UP to a multiple of the frame rate. At fraction 0.5
     * the request is 181 and the run takes 192; at 0.125 the request is 362 and the run takes
     * 384; at 0.25 the request is 256 and the two agree, which is worse than a constant offset
     * because it makes the discrepancy intermittent.
     *
     * The page already refuses to echo the requested SCHEME back at the reader — that is why
     * `chooseIntegrator` reports its pick. Echoing the requested step rate was the same mistake
     * one field to the right.
     */
    stepsPerTCross: number | null;
  }

  /** Wraps a force model to count evaluations — the cost readout, without touching the core. */
  function counted(force: ForceModel): { force: ForceModel; evals: () => number } {
    let n = 0;
    const bump = () => {
      n++;
    };
    return {
      evals: () => n,
      force: {
        id: force.id,
        accelerations: (p, m, a, t) => {
          bump();
          force.accelerations(p, m, a, t);
        },
        potentialEnergy: (p, m, t) => force.potentialEnergy(p, m, t),
        potentials: (p, m, o, t) => force.potentials(p, m, o, t),
        forceGradient: force.forceGradient
          ? (p, m, a, g, t) => {
              bump();
              force.forceGradient!(p, m, a, g, t);
            }
          : undefined,
        accelerationsAndJerk: force.accelerationsAndJerk
          ? (
              p: Vec3Array,
              v: Vec3Array,
              m: Float64Array,
              a: Vec3Array,
              j: Vec3Array,
              t: number,
            ) => {
              bump();
              force.accelerationsAndJerk!(p, v, m, a, j, t);
            }
          : undefined,
      },
    };
  }

  let run: Run | null = null;
  let running = true;
  let lastEvals = 0;
  let evalsPerFrame = 0;

  function build(): Run {
    const fraction = FRACTIONS[Number(epsInput.value)];
    const n = Number(nInput.value);
    const scheme = (
      document.querySelector<HTMLInputElement>('input[name="scheme"]:checked')?.value ?? "fsi4"
    ) as Scheme;

    const softening = softeningForCluster(R_HALF, n, fraction);
    /* The IC's virial scaling must use the SAME force law that will step it, or the cluster
       starts at a Q it is not actually at. `clusterState` takes the model for that reason. */
    const bare = createDirectForce({ softening });
    const state = clusterState(
      defaultIdentity({
        seed: 2026,
        sampling: { mode: "count", target: n },
        profile: { kind: "plummer", scaleRadius: SCALE_PC },
        kinematics: { virialRatio: 0.5 },
      }),
      bare,
    );

    const { force, evals } = counted(bare);
    const tCross = crossingTime(state);
    /* Fixed-step schemes get the step derived from the softening — the two are coupled, and
       holding one while changing the other is the confound that produced a wrong conclusion
       once already. Hermite sizes its own and only takes a ceiling. */
    const requested = stepsForSoftening(fraction);
    const maxStep = tCross / requested;
    const adaptive = scheme === "hermite";
    const picked = chooseIntegrator(state, force, {
      prefer: scheme,
      maxStep: adaptive ? tCross / FRAMES_PER_TCROSS : maxStep,
      adaptive,
    });

    return {
      state,
      force,
      integrator: picked.integrator,
      monitor: createConservationMonitor(picked.integrator),
      tCross,
      scheme: picked.scheme,
      evals,
      stepsPerTCross: adaptive
        ? null
        : Math.ceil(requested / FRAMES_PER_TCROSS) * FRAMES_PER_TCROSS,
    };
  }

  function reset(): void {
    run = build();
    lastEvals = 0;
    evalsPerFrame = 0;
    const fraction = FRACTIONS[Number(epsInput.value)];
    el<HTMLElement>("[data-eps-label]").textContent = `${fraction} d`;
    el<HTMLElement>("[data-n-label]").textContent = nInput.value;
    /* The RUNNING scheme is named here, from `chooseIntegrator`'s own report rather than from the
       radio that was clicked. Those can differ — that is the whole reason `chooseIntegrator`
       returns what it picked — and a page that echoed the request instead of the result would be
       the confidently-wrong readout this codebase keeps designing against. */
    const rate =
      run.stepsPerTCross === null
        ? `adaptive step (asked for ${stepsForSoftening(fraction)}/t_cross as a ceiling)`
        : `${run.stepsPerTCross} steps/t_cross` +
          (run.stepsPerTCross === stepsForSoftening(fraction)
            ? ""
            : ` (softening asked for ${stepsForSoftening(fraction)}; the frame subdivides to an integer)`);
    status.textContent =
      `${run.scheme} · ${run.state.n} stars · ε = ${softeningForCluster(R_HALF, run.state.n, fraction).toFixed(4)} pc` +
      ` · t_cross = ${run.tCross.toFixed(3)} Myr · ${rate}` +
      ` · available: ${availableSchemes(run.force).join(", ")}`;
    /* Refresh the panel NOW rather than waiting for the next animation frame. While paused there
       is no next frame at all, so without this a control change left every number describing the
       previous configuration — and at fine softening, where a frame can take half a second, it
       did so for long enough to read as a result. */
    draw();
    readout();
  }

  const fmt = (x: number): string => (x === 0 ? "0" : x.toExponential(2));

  function draw(): void {
    if (!run) return;
    const { width: w, height: h } = canvas;
    const dpr = w / Math.max(1, canvas.getBoundingClientRect().width);
    ctx.clearRect(0, 0, w, h);
    const scale = Math.min(w, h) / (2 * VIEW_PC);
    const { pos, mass, n } = run.state;
    for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
      const x = w / 2 + pos[i * 3] * scale;
      const y = h / 2 + pos[i * 3 + 1] * scale;
      /* Radius from mass, not from a look. Cube root so the AREA scales with mass^(2/3) and a
         20 Msun star does not blot out its neighbours; the point is to see segregation.
         SCALED BY dpr, because the canvas is drawn in DEVICE pixels: without this a 1 Msun star
         came out 1.1 device px = 0.55 CSS px on a retina display, i.e. sub-pixel and invisible. */
      const r = Math.max(0.6, Math.cbrt(mass[i]) * 1.4) * dpr;
      // Depth cue only: nearer stars brighter. No physics is being asserted by the colour.
      const z = pos[i * 3 + 2];
      const a = Math.max(0.35, Math.min(1, 0.8 + z / (2 * VIEW_PC)));
      ctx.fillStyle = `rgba(232, 240, 248, ${a})`;
      ctx.beginPath();
      ctx.arc(x, y, r, 0, Math.PI * 2);
      ctx.fill();
    }
  }

  function readout(): void {
    if (!run) return;
    const s = run.monitor.sample();
    const d = measure(run.state, run.force);
    el<HTMLElement>("[data-de]").textContent = fmt(s.energyDrift);
    el<HTMLElement>("[data-dew]").textContent = fmt(run.monitor.worst.energy);
    el<HTMLElement>("[data-q]").textContent = s.virialRatio.toFixed(3);
    el<HTMLElement>("[data-tc]").textContent = s.tCross.toFixed(2);
    el<HTMLElement>("[data-bound]").textContent = `${(d.boundMassFraction * 100).toFixed(1)}%`;
    el<HTMLElement>("[data-cost]").textContent = evalsPerFrame.toFixed(0);

    const verdict = el<HTMLElement>("[data-health]");
    verdict.textContent = run.monitor.healthy
      ? `${run.scheme}: energy held inside ${fmt(run.monitor.energyTolerance)}`
      : `${run.scheme}: energy drift EXCEEDED ${fmt(run.monitor.energyTolerance)} — this run is not trustworthy`;
    verdict.dataset.bad = String(!run.monitor.healthy);
  }

  /* Sized from a ResizeObserver rather than a window `resize` listener. The window event does not
     fire for the INITIAL layout, so a one-shot call at startup measured the canvas before CSS had
     given it a width: the backing buffer came out 278 px inside an 877 px box, and everything drew
     into the top-left ninth and was stretched back out. The observer fires on first layout and on
     every subsequent change, which is the actual thing being tracked. */
  function resize(): void {
    const dpr = Math.min(window.devicePixelRatio || 1, 2);
    const rect = canvas.getBoundingClientRect();
    const w = Math.max(1, Math.round(rect.width * dpr));
    const h = Math.max(1, Math.round(rect.height * dpr));
    if (canvas.width === w && canvas.height === h) return;
    canvas.width = w;
    canvas.height = h;
    draw();
  }

  function tick(): void {
    requestAnimationFrame(tick);
    if (!run || !running) return;
    try {
      run.integrator.step(run.tCross / 64);
    } catch (err) {
      running = false;
      playBtn.textContent = "Play";
      status.textContent = `Stopped: ${(err as Error).message}`;
      return;
    }
    const now = run.evals();
    evalsPerFrame = now - lastEvals;
    lastEvals = now;
    draw();
    readout();
  }

  playBtn.addEventListener("click", () => {
    running = !running;
    playBtn.textContent = running ? "Pause" : "Play";
    playBtn.setAttribute("aria-pressed", String(running));
  });
  resetBtn.addEventListener("click", reset);
  for (const input of [epsInput, nInput]) input.addEventListener("input", reset);
  for (const radio of document.querySelectorAll('input[name="scheme"]')) {
    radio.addEventListener("change", reset);
  }
  new ResizeObserver(resize).observe(canvas);

  /* Respect the OS setting: start paused rather than autoplaying motion. The control is visible
     either way, so the page is never a thing that moves with no way to stop it. */
  if (window.matchMedia("(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)").matches) {
    running = false;
    playBtn.textContent = "Play";
    playBtn.setAttribute("aria-pressed", "false");
  }

  reset();
  resize();
  readout();
  tick();
</script>

<style>
  .lab {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 2fr) minmax(16rem, 1fr);
    gap: var(--space-m);
    padding-block: var(--space-l);
  }
  @media (width < 60rem) {
    .lab {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }
  }
  .lab__canvas {
    inline-size: 100%;
    aspect-ratio: 4 / 3;
    display: block;
    background: radial-gradient(120% 120% at 50% 45%, #0c1114, var(--bg-void));
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: var(--radius-m);
  }
  .lab__note,
  .ctl__hint {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--step--1);
    color: var(--ink-dim);
    margin-block-start: var(--space-2xs);
  }
  .ctl {
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: var(--radius-m);
    padding: var(--space-xs) var(--space-s) var(--space-s);
  }
  .ctl legend {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--step--1);
    color: var(--ink-dim);
    padding-inline: var(--space-3xs);
  }
  .ctl__val {
    color: var(--ink);
  }
  .ctl__row {
    display: flex;
    gap: var(--space-2xs);
    align-items: baseline;
    font-size: var(--step--1);
    padding-block: var(--space-3xs);
  }
  .ctl input[type="range"] {
    inline-size: 100%;
  }
  .ctl--buttons {
    display: flex;
    gap: var(--space-2xs);
    border: 0;
    padding: 0;
  }
  .ctl--buttons button {
    flex: 1;
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--step--1);
    padding: var(--space-2xs);
    background: var(--bg-surface);
    color: var(--ink);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: var(--radius-s);
    cursor: pointer;
  }
  .meter {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--step--1);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: var(--radius-m);
    padding: var(--space-s);
  }
  .meter__row {
    display: flex;
    justify-content: space-between;
    gap: var(--space-s);
  }
  .meter__row dt {
    color: var(--ink-dim);
  }
  .meter__row dd {
    margin: 0;
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  }
  .meter__verdict {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--step--1);
    color: var(--ink-dim);
  }
  .meter__verdict[data-bad="true"] {
    color: var(--spec-rose-bright);
  }
  .tbl {
    inline-size: auto;
    min-inline-size: min(100%, 36rem);
    border-collapse: collapse;
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--step--1);
    margin-block: var(--space-m);
  }
  .tbl caption {
    text-align: start;
    color: var(--ink-dim);
    padding-block-end: var(--space-2xs);
  }
  .tbl th,
  .tbl td {
    text-align: end;
    padding: var(--space-3xs) var(--space-2xs);
    border-block-end: 1px solid var(--border);
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  }
  .tbl thead th,
  .tbl tbody th {
    text-align: start;
    color: var(--ink-dim);
    font-weight: 400;
  }
</style>
